module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Noetherian.Defs
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.AdicCompletion.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Maps

import Mathlib.RingTheory.AdicCompletion.LocalRing
import Mathlib.RingTheory.AdicCompletion.AsTensorProduct
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Nakayama
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.FaithfullyFlat.Algebra
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.FiniteOverCompleteRing
import Sheaf.RingTheory.AdicCompleteSubideal

/-! # Adic completeness for a second ideal

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0DYC (lemma-complete-modulo-nilpotent)
(blueprint: lem-sfb-two-ideal-adic-completeness).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

/-- A Noetherian ring complete for `I` is complete for `J` when its quotient by `I`
is complete for the image of `J`, namely `(I + J) / I`.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0DYC (lemma-complete-modulo-nilpotent).
(blueprint: lem-sfb-two-ideal-adic-completeness)

Proof:
1. Set `K = I + J` and let `c : R → B` be the canonical `K`-adic completion map,
   using def-weil-pbc-adic-completion. All three ideals are finitely generated by
   def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring. By lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-ideal, `B` is
   complete for `KB`. By lem-weil-pbc-completion-finite-module-tensor, the completion
   of the cyclic module `R/I` is `B ⊗[R] (R/I)`. Identify this tensor product with
   `B/IB` by `b ⊗ r̄ ↦ b c(r) mod IB`, with inverse `b mod IB ↦ b ⊗ 1`.
   The powers of `K` act on `R/I` as powers of the image of `J`, so quotient
   completeness identifies the canonical map `R/I → B/IB` with an isomorphism.
2. The ideal `IB` is finitely generated and contained in `KB`. Apply
   lem-sfb-adic-complete-subideal to make `B` complete for `IB`; in particular
   the intersection of `I^n B` is zero by def-weil-pbc-adically-complete-ring.
   Apply lem-weil-pbc-finite-over-complete-ring: `R` is `I`-complete and
   `B/IB ≅ R/I` is cyclic, so `B` is finite as an `R`-module.
3. By lem-weil-pbc-adic-complete-jacobson-radical, `I` lies in the Jacobson
   radical of `R`. The finite module `C = B/c(R)` satisfies `IC = C`, since
   `R/I → B/IB` is surjective. Apply lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-jacobson to get
   `C = 0`, proving that `c` is surjective.
4. By lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-completion-flat, `B` is flat over `R`. For every
   maximal ideal `m`, we have `I ⊆ m` and `B/mB ≅ R/m ≠ 0`, using the canonical
   quotient identification of step 1. By
   lem-weil-pbc-flat-module-faithfully-flat-maximal-ideals, `B` is faithfully flat.
   Apply lem-weil-pbc-faithfully-flat-ideal-contraction to the zero ideal to
   conclude that `c` is injective, hence bijective.
5. This bijectivity is `K`-adic completeness by def-weil-pbc-adically-complete-ring.
   As `J ⊆ K` and `J` is finitely generated, lem-sfb-adic-complete-subideal
   gives `J`-adic completeness of `R`.
-/
theorem IsAdicComplete.of_quotient_complete (R : Type u) [CommRing R]
    [IsNoetherianRing R] (I J : Ideal R) [IsAdicComplete I R]
    [IsAdicComplete (J.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk I)) (R ⧸ I)] :
    IsAdicComplete J R := by
  sorry
